description:
Cleanup that follows ox-x60c. NOT RAII — Go doesn't have RAII, and the leaked FDs are owned by fsnotify's private state, not ours. This is a discipline helper: a typed wrapper that pairs watcher.Add + os.ReadDir snapshot + Remove-with-children in one place, so a future caller can't write watcher.Add(path) without also registering for the per-child cleanup. The caller still has to call Release() — there is no scope-bound auto-release in Go that would work for the daemon's long-lived watcher.

type watchedDirHandle struct {
    watcher FileSystemWatcher
    path    string
    pw      *ProjectWatcher
}

func (pw *ProjectWatcher) acquireDir(watcher FileSystemWatcher, path string) (*watchedDirHandle, error) {
    if err := watcher.Add(path); err != nil { return nil, err }
    children := snapshotDirFiles(pw.fs, path)
    pw.mu.Lock()
    pw.watchedDirs[path] = struct{}{}
    pw.dirChildren[path] = children
    pw.mu.Unlock()
    return &watchedDirHandle{watcher, path, pw}, nil
}

func (h *watchedDirHandle) Release() {
    h.pw.mu.Lock()
    children := h.pw.dirChildren[h.path]
    delete(h.pw.dirChildren, h.path)
    delete(h.pw.watchedDirs, h.path)
    h.pw.mu.Unlock()
    for _, f := range children { _ = h.watcher.Remove(f) }
    _ = h.watcher.Remove(h.path)
}

Value: concentrates cleanup logic so a future engineer adding a new watch site can't forget to register children for cleanup. Stronger variant worth considering: have acquireDir take a context.Context and auto-Release when ctx is cancelled, so daemon shutdown gives a deterministic cleanup path. Optional cleanup — the core leak fix in ox-x60c is steps 1–4 of the implementation. This issue is purely code-organization.
